{
  "data": {
    "text": "Effects of a Motor Imagery Task on Functional Brain Network Community Structure in Older Adults: Data from the Brain Networks and Mobility Function (B-NET) Study\n\nBrain Sci. 2021, 11, 118. https://doi.org/10.3390/brainsci11010118\n\nPublisher’s Note: MDPI stays neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\n\nCopyright: © 2021 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).\n\nGlobal brain network community structure decreased during the MI task when compared to the resting state.\n\nData were from the Brain Networks and Mobility (B-NET) study (NCT03430427). B-NET is an ongoing longitudinal, observational trial of community-dwelling older adults aged 70 and older recruited from Forsyth County, NC and the surrounding regions.\n\nThe analysis presented here includes 157 participants with complete imaging data at baseline. Participants (n = 157) were from the Brain Networks and Mobility (B-NET) Study (mean age = 76.1 ± 4.3; % female = 55.4; % African American = 8.3; mean years of education = 15.7 ± 2.5).\n\nThe study was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) of the Wake Forest School of Medicine (IRB Protocol No.: IRB00046460; approval date: 27 August 2020).\n\nStimulus presentation consisted of videos from the Mobility Assessment Tool–short form (MAT-sf) that were played through a standard media player. Participants were instructed to visualize themselves as the avatar in the videos as it completed a series of movements.\n\nTwo videos were created using the MAT-sf stimuli, one showing mobility tasks that are relatively easy (Easy Task) and the other showing more difficult mobility tasks (Hard Task). Each task was administered for 260 s, where each stimulus was repeated 5 times.\n\nAll brain images were collected on a Siemens 3T Skyra MRI Scanner equipped with a 32-channel head coil. Structural scans included a T1-weighted anatomical image (TR = 2300 msec; TE = 2.98 msec; number of slices = 192; slice thickness = 1.0 mm; voxel dimensions = 1.0 × 1.0 × 1.0 mm; FOV = 256 mm; scan duration = 312 s) obtained using 3D volumetric MPRAGE. Blood oxygenation level-dependent (BOLD) imaging (TR = 2000 ms; TE = 25 ms; number of slices = 35; slice thickness = 5.0 mm; voxel dimensions = 4.0 × 4.0 × 5.0 mm; FOV = 256 mm) was collected at rest (scan duration = 460 s) and during two motor imagery tasks (260 s each).\n\nStructural image segmentation, including the removal of nonbrain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), was completed using Statistical Parametric Mapping version 12 (SPM12, http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm). A whole-brain mask was created for each subject, using the white and gray matter segments, to improve the coregistration of the functional and anatomical images. All images were inspected and manually cleaned to remove any extraparenchymal tissues using the MRIcron software (https://www.nitrc.org/projects/mricron). High-resolution T1-weighted images were spatially normalized to the Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) template using Advanced Normalization Tools (ANTs). Distortion correction was completed using FMRIB’s Software Library (FSL). The first 10 volumes of the BOLD images were dropped to allow for signal normalization. Slice time correction and realignment of the functional images were completed using SPM12. The BOLD images were coregistered to the native-space anatomical images and warped to MNI space using the transformation derived from ANTs. Head motion was additionally corrected for with motion scrubbing. Finally, data were band-pass-filtered (0.009–0.08 Hz) to account for low-frequency drift and physiologic noise. Confounding signals were regressed out from the filtered data, including signals from white matter, gray matter, CSF, and 6 rigid-body motion parameters generated during the realignment process.\n\nThe presence of a connection between two nodes (i and j) was determined by completing a time series regression analysis. This produced a cross-correlation matrix, representing the Pearson’s correlation coefficient between every network node. A threshold was chosen to dichotomize the data and create the final binary adjacency matrix where values above the threshold indicate a connection. Our networks were thresholded at S = 2.5. Community structure analyses were performed to assign each voxel to a network community using network modularity (Q), maximizing Q with a dynamic Markov process. Scaled Inclusivity (SI) was used to compute the spatial consistency of community structure across the group.\n\nFollowing each video, the participants completed a short survey with three questions on their ability to imagine themselves completing the task… Answers were collected on the visual analog scale (VAS), which included a slider with values ranging between 0 and 100.\n\nData Availability Statement: The data presented in this study are available upon request from the authors.\n\nThe presence of a connection between two nodes (i and j) was determined by completing a time series regression analysis. This produced a cross-correlation matrix, which represents the Pearson's correlation coefficient that describes the connectivity between every network node. A threshold was then chosen to dichotomize the data and create the final binary adjacency matrix (Aij) where values above the threshold indicate a connection is present. The threshold ensures that the density of connections was comparable across all the participants. More specifically, S = log (N)/log (K) is conserved across the participants. Our networks were thresholded at S = 2.5 based on previous work.\n\nThe strength of community structures was identified using network modularity or Q. A dynamic Markov process was used to maximize Q and partition the brain networks into communities. It was run 100 times, and the partition associated with the highest Q value was chosen."
  },
